// Ryan Yu's Sky Striker, 1st place and UNDEFEATED (18-0) at the North America
// WCQ 2026 (Minneapolis, 11 Jul 2026, 2,077 duelists). The 40/15/15 below is
// transcribed from https://ygoprodeck.com/deck/sky-striker-721413 and confirmed
// card-for-card against Konami's official Top 32 decklists (as "Ryan Linus ON
// Yu - 1"). Every card name verified verbatim against vendor/BabelCDB/cards.cdb
// via `node bin/ygo.js card`. NOTE the exact names: "Sky Striker Ace = Zero"
// (equals sign, not a hyphen) and "S:P Little Knight" (colon, no spaces).
//
// This is a DIFFERENT deck from `cimoooooooo-sky-striker.json`, not a variant:
// no Hornet Drones, no Multirole, no Area Zero, no Traps at all. See the manual.
//
// ⚠ ENGINE LIMITATION, NOT A DECKLIST PROBLEM (found while play-testing this
// deck, 2026-08-17). Three cards in this list can hit a pre-existing crash in
// the vendored scripts: `vendor/CardScripts/chain.lua` wraps `Duel.RegisterEffect`
// (line ~577) to snapshot the reason-effect's card properties, and that snapshot
// queries EVERY CHAININFO_TRIGGERING_* flag, including the two highest
// (LSCALE=32 / RSCALE=33) that the pinned ocgcore-wasm 0.1.2 core rejects with
// "Passed invalid CHAININFO flag". Any card that registers such an effect from
// inside a resolving effect can trip it:
//   - `Droll & Lock Bird` — ALWAYS (its operation registers EFFECT_CANNOT_TO_HAND).
//     The decision is refused and the duel deadlocks unless the Droll is negated.
//   - `Sky Striker Mobilize - Linkage!` — when its own card is destroyed while it
//     resolves (e.g. `The Fallen & The Virtuous` or `Mystical Space Typhoon` with
//     Linkage as the only face-up target). Deadlocks if that destroy is forced.
//   - `Sky Striker Ace - Zeke` — its Link-Summon banish; recoverable, because
//     other menu options stay legal.
// No other shipped deck runs Droll, so this deck is the first to expose it. The
// list is kept faithful to the tournament decklist; the fix belongs in chain.lua
// (drop "Scale" from CARD_PROPERTIES, or pcall the chaininfo getter).
{
  "name": "Ryan Yu Sky Striker",
  "category": "curated",
  "format": "classic",
  "manual": "GLOSSARY. **MMZ** = your five Main Monster Zones (front row). **EMZ** = the two Extra Monster Zones shared by both players; you may occupy only ONE at a time, so a Sky Striker board is ONE Ace up top plus Set Quick-Plays plus hand traps — never a wall of monsters. **Ace** = any monster whose name contains \"Sky Striker Ace\", which includes `Prototype Sky Striker Ace - Amatsu`. **The 3+ threshold** = \"if you have 3 or more Spells in your GY\", the clause that upgrades `Engage!` (draw), `Widow Anchor` (steal) and `Shark Cannon` (revive). **Tag out** = Tribute `Raye` (or `Zero`) at Quick speed to trade it for a different Ace. **Clip** = an opponent banishing a face-down card out of your Extra Deck (the Kewl Tune card of that name does exactly this); more loosely, any Ace you lose forever — Extra Deck monsters that leave the field never come back.\n\n=== BIG PICTURE ===\nKeep your MAIN MONSTER ZONE EMPTY, hold Quick-Play Spells, play on BOTH turns, win on card quality. Almost every Sky Striker card reads \"If you control no monsters in your Main Monster Zone\" — but an Ace sitting in the EXTRA Monster Zone does NOT switch your Spells off (`Engage!`'s script checks only monster-zone sequences 0-4). So every turn has the same shape: ONE Ace in the EMZ, MMZ empty, Spells piling into the GY.\nEverything snowballs on ONE number: **Spells in your GY**. This 40 is 27 Spells, 13 monsters, ZERO Traps, and 19 of those Spells are Quick-Play. Ryan Yu, who went 18-0 and won the North America WCQ 2026 (2,077 duelists, Minneapolis) with this exact 40/15/15: \"this deck cares a lot about getting spells in grave fast. **There's a huge difference between Engage in grave with one or two spells versus zero spells.**\"\nContext for how much of this is the deck and how much is the pilot — his own answer, on the On the Bubble podcast a week later: \"people [call] me like a Sky Striker one trick or whatever. But **I can literally play this well with anything I want**, so... **it's not the deck.**\"\n\n=== HOW THIS BUILD DIFFERS FROM CLASSIC SKY STRIKER (compare `cimoooooooo-sky-striker` in this repo) ===\nGONE: `Hornet Drones`, `Multirole`, `Area Zero`, `Hercules Base`, `Afterburners!`, `Jamming Waves!`, `Terraforming`, `Kaina`, `Azalea Temperance`, `Infinite Impermanence`, and every Trap. No Token, no Field Spell, no End-Phase re-Set engine. **Do not look for them in the menu.** Your only Link fodder is `Raye`, `Roze`, `Linkage!`, and monsters you steal.\n- **It is a DRAW deck, not a backrow deck.** 3 `Pot of Desires` + 3 `Radiant Typhoon Vision` + 1 `Mystical Space Typhoon`. Ryan: \"**Desires is way too broken not to play.** Any card that accelerates your engine is just what you need.\" Ranking the non-engine above the archetype's own best card: \"three Desires, three Vision, MST. **These are the best six cards in the deck. These are literally better than Engage.** At least when you're starting, these are way more important to see.\"\n- **Eight hand traps instead of backrow** (3 `Ash Blossom`, 2 `Ghost Ogre`, 1 `Ghost Belle`, 2 `Droll & Lock Bird`). Ryan: \"This is pretty unconventional for a Sky Striker deck... **having hand traps in this deck is really important, especially post side, because they're a main deck disruption that can't get immediately outed by Lightning Storm or Feather Duster.** So whenever I had a little half board — maybe a little Anchor with Linkage — plus one or two hand traps, I felt much more safe than if I'm full committing to backrow and just praying they don't have Feather [Duster].\" DECISION RULE: **never dump your hand into Set cards. Two pieces of interaction plus two hand traps beats a five-card backrow.**\n  He arrived at this by CUTTING 3 `Upstart Goblin` from his YCS Columbus list (Top 32, May 2026) for the 2 `Ghost Ogre` + 1 `Ghost Belle`. An analyst's read on why: \"**he chose to play Sky Striker because all of you chose to not play Droll.** Because otherwise, if Droll were in the meta and you're playing Sky Striker, you don't play Upstart Goblin.\"\n- **No \"Radiant Typhoon\" MONSTERS, only the Spell.** Ryan: \"it's pure Sky Striker, no main deck [Radiant] Typhoon [monster] cards. **Those cards are just completely unusable going second because they become normal summons — you just can't get it out of your hand.** I don't want to have to burn an MST [to turn them on]... those cards are also vulnerable to Impermanence and Veiler. They're nice for consistency, but the card quality just isn't good enough to play them. So just a pure engine.\" (The Radiant Typhoon monsters only Special Summon themselves if `Mystical Space Typhoon` is in your GY or the opponent controls no Spells/Traps — both usually false.) Note the single `Mystical Space Typhoon` was added AFTER a Master Duel run where its absence hurt: \"the one downside is the fact that **there isn't a search target for Vision. Not having an MST came up quite often.**\"\n- **Deliberately LOW counts on situational cards.** `Forbidden Crown` went 3 -> 2 between Columbus and the WCQ. Ryan: \"you could play it at three, but **the worst thing that can happen in this deck is that you're deep drawing into multiples of cards, so you have dead cards in your hand. That's how you lose** — if you have not enough cards in your hand that do different things. And that's also part of why playing these hand trap ratios is a little bit better, like two-one. I had games where I would draw one of each of these and it wins me the game instead of like three [Ghost Ogre] or three [Belle].\" EVALUATION RULE: **count how many DIFFERENT things your hand does, not how many good cards it holds.**\n- **3 `Sky Striker Ace = Zero` / 3 `Sky Striker Ace - Kagari`.** Ryan: \"Three Zeros, three Kagari... **I actually do not understand why most people play three and two of some ratio. Some people even play two and two, that's completely crazy to me. Three of each is mandatory. It's not a skill issue if you need a third one of each.** Sometimes when you're grinding you want to conserve follow-up — but to do that, ironically, **you need to burn through these. You need to play more aggressively with them.** I'll use a Kagari more willingly to guarantee I follow up cuz I know there's another one in my extra deck. And also **this is clip insurance** — I had so many games where my opponent would clip a Zero or clip a Kagari, and if I don't have three of each I just would lose the game.\" (The literal threat: the dominant deck of that format banishes a random face-down card out of your Extra Deck as a Synchro-material effect.) A second regional-winning pilot: \"I played a Sky Striker mirror match where **my opponent lost because they only played two Kagaris**... I personally think I would cut a Zero before I would cut a Kagari.\"\n\n=== THE TRICK THAT MAKES THE WHOLE DECK WORK: CHAIN A SPELL TO YOUR OWN SPELL ===\n`Engage!`'s bonus draw checks \"3 or more Spells in your GY\" **at resolution**, not on activation, and a Spell that finishes resolving hits the GY BEFORE the next chain link resolves. So **chaining a Quick-Play Spell on top of your own `Engage!` turns a no-draw Engage into a draw.** Ryan says exactly this about `The Fallen & The Virtuous`: \"it's a quick play for Vision, and **lets you chain to Engage to get more spells in grave**.\" A Sky Striker creator gives the extreme version — a Spell can be its OWN third Spell: \"we are going to chain our MST, popping our own Engage. So MST popped Engage, and now when Engage resolves, **Engage will be its own third spell card.**\"\nENGINE-VERIFIED in this exact build: from the opener `Pot of Desires` / `Engage!` / `Radiant Typhoon Vision` / `The Fallen & The Virtuous` / `Raye`, the line `Desires` -> `Engage!` (CL1) + chain `Vision` (CL2) + chain `The Fallen & The Virtuous` (CL3, destroying your OWN face-up Engage) resolves backwards into: Albion sent to GY, Engage destroyed into the GY, Vision draws 2 and discards a Quick-Play into the GY, Vision to GY — five Spells in the GY — and `Engage!` then searches AND draws. **Whenever you hold a spare Quick-Play and Engage would not otherwise draw, chain it.**\n\n=== TURN 1 (going first) ===\nTarget endboard — the consensus across every source, and what this list actually builds: **`Sky Striker Ace = Zero` alone in an Extra Monster Zone, MMZ empty, a Set `Widow Anchor` and a Set `Linkage!` and/or `Lemnisgate!`, `Raye` (ideally `Roze` too) in the GY, `Engage!` in the GY for Kagari follow-up, 3+ Spells in the GY, and hand traps still in hand.** In his own WCQ Top 4, Duel 3 — the one duel he chose to go first — he set up Kagari -> Zero -> Shizuku with three backrow and then simply `Forbidden Crown`ed the opponent's Normal Summon for the concession.\n1. **Fire the draw Spells first.** `Pot of Desires` and `Radiant Typhoon Vision` before committing a monster: they bank Spells and find the engine. `Vision` -> draw 2, then discard a Quick-Play Spell from hand — **you choose the discard AFTER seeing the two cards** (\"very importantly, you do get to make the choice of discarding after you've drawn the cards. So if you see a redundant card in your hand after you've drawn, you can choose to discard that\"). Never fire it with no Quick-Play at all: the card then makes you **discard your ENTIRE hand**. With 18 other Quick-Plays in this 40 you are far past the sourced safety line (~\"if you want that 85% chance you're going to want to run 10... 90%, run 12 or more\").\n2. **The one-card `Raye` line:** Normal Summon `Raye` -> Quick Effect, Tribute her -> Special Summon `Sky Striker Ace = Zero` to the EMZ -> Zero's summon trigger adds 1 \"Sky Striker\" **Spell** from Deck OR GY. \"we can normal summon Raye. Raye can use her tag out effect and go into our Ace to Zero. So we're going to use the first effect of Ace to Zero to grab the second most important card in our deck, Sky Striker Mobilize Engage. Then we can use Engage.\"\n   RAYE CAN ONLY REACH `Zero`, `Kagari`, `Shizuku`, `Hayate`, `Amatsu`. \"one of the top questions I get is why could Raye not tag out into Zeke, Azalea... **the very first line of all three of these cards states 'Must first be Link Summoned'**... cards like Linkage and Raye cannot bring these cards out.\" (Engine-confirmed: Raye's menu offers exactly those five.)\n3. **The one-card `Roze` line** — Roze is a Level 4 you may Normal Summon: \"use Roze, link off our Roze into Shizuku, and that's pretty much it. Then... **in the end phase we do the new-age version of the Shizuku shuffle. Shizuku will add say Linkage to our hand, and then in the end phase we can use Linkage sending away our Shizuku, going into Ace Zero. Ace Zero can then resolve grabbing us an Engage for our follow-up turn.**\" Quick-Play Spells ARE usable in the End Phase — \"someone tried to call me out saying that I couldn't activate spell cards in the end phase, but you can.\"\n4. **What `Engage!` searches:** \"Typically, my Engage — first Engage search — **always grabs Linkage**, just because you can also use Linkage to dodge hand traps.\" Second priority `Widow Anchor`; `Lemnisgate!` only once your GY holds Aces AND Spells to shuffle. `Engage!` searches any Sky Striker CARD, so `Raye`/`Roze` are legal grabs when you need a body.\n5. **`Linkage!` is a STARTER, not just an extender.** It needs (a) an empty MMZ at activation and (b) any other card you control to send. \"you can have any blank card, just any random card, **set the card, use Linkage, send it away**, and then go into cards like Kagari or Ace Zero, and basically **use Linkage as a starter**.\" Better: chain it to your own Spell and send THAT card — \"use your [Normal Spell], **chain your Linkage to send it away**, go into your Ace Zero, and then [the Normal Spell] will resolve... **the card we're sending can be a card we're already using with Linkage.**\" (Engine-confirmed: `Linkage!` appears as a response to your own Spell activation and can send the card that is resolving.)\n   `Linkage!` gives the summoned Ace **+1000 ATK if you have at least 1 LIGHT and 1 DARK \"Sky Striker Ace\" on field and/or in GY**. Zero is LIGHT; the DARK is `Raye`, `Amatsu`, `Zeke`, `Azalea` or `Camellia`. \"because we used Linkage our Zero now gets a 1K attack boost because it is a light monster, and we have Amatsu, which is a dark monster in the graveyard — **which is why we went into Amatsu earlier**.\"\n   ⚠ **`Linkage!` locks you out of every non-\"Sky Striker Ace\" Extra Deck monster for the rest of the turn.** \"if you use Linkage, you cannot go into S:P Little Knight afterwards.\" **If you want `S:P Little Knight` this turn, make it BEFORE Linkage! resolves.**\n6. **`Zero` gets ONE effect per turn — search OR tag out, never both.** \"if you use the search with Ace Zero, **you cannot use the tag out effect in the same turn. Which is why going first, use the search effect, keep Ace Zero as a body on board, and on your opponent's turn use the tag out effect.**\" With 3 copies you still get one Zero effect per TURN, not per copy.\n7. **Set only what you will actually use on their turn.** A Quick-Play can be activated from the hand only on YOUR turn; to use `Widow Anchor`, `Linkage!`, `Lemnisgate!`, `Forbidden Crown`, `Shark Cannon` or `The Fallen & The Virtuous` during the opponent's turn it must already be Set. Balance that against the \"don't full-commit to backrow\" rule — two Sets is usually right.\n8. **`Camellia` as a deliberate end-board piece.** If `Engage!` already found `Linkage!`, Link Summon `Camellia` (2 Effect Monsters; `Raye` + `Roze` works) and search by milling instead: \"**using Camellia as an end board piece, really good, because I was able to set the Widow Anchor.**\" Then on their turn `Linkage!` sends Camellia away — see the Camellia loop below.\n\n=== THE OPPONENT'S TURN IS YOUR REAL TURN ===\n1. **`Widow Anchor` first** — negate a face-up Effect Monster, and at 3+ Spells take it too. \"we probably want to prioritize using our Widow Anchor with the negate, using that first. So that's one interruption.\"\n   ⚠ The stolen monster lands in YOUR MMZ and switches your Spells OFF. Cash it in immediately: 2 Effect Monsters -> `Camellia` or `S:P Little Knight`; 2 monsters incl. an Ace -> `Zeke`; 2 LIGHT/DARK -> `Azalea`. \"being able to use Widow Anchor to snatch one of your opponent's monsters and then **link it off for Camellia** — underrated.\"\n   ⭐ SEQUENCING, from an analyst reviewing Ryan's own rank-1 replays: \"**It's Linkage and Widow Anchor where you need to have no monsters in the main monster zone to use... so we have to make sure that we don't take control with the FIRST Widow Anchor, and we don't activate Zero before being able to use our other Widow Anchor.** He just wants to make the most use out of his second Widow Anchor.\" I.e. **negate-only with the first Anchor, steal with the last one.**\n   MIRROR TECH: \"if your opponent tries to use Widow Anchor and you know they have three spells in the graveyard to take one of your link monsters... **sometimes it's more optimal to negate your own link monster.** That way they do not get a body on the field.\"\n   AND against an unaffected monster, fire it anyway — \"you can use the Widow Anchor on the unaffected monster **just to get it into the graveyard**\" (purely for the Spell count).\n2. **Then `Zero`'s tag out** — Tribute Zero: Special Summon `Raye` AND `Roze` from Deck and/or GY, then destroy 1 card on the field.\n3. **`Lemnisgate!` in the GY turns that into a DOUBLE pop.** Its GY effect: when a \"Sky Striker\" monster is Special Summoned to your field, banish `Lemnisgate!` and immediately Link Summon 1 \"Sky Striker Ace\" Link Monster. \"**when we have Lemnisgate in the graveyard it becomes a lot better.** Ace Zero tags itself out, go into your Raye and Roze, pop one of your opponent's monsters, then on summon of Raye and Roze you can trigger your Lemnisgate to banish itself, and then **use the Raye and Roze to go into a card like Azalea for an additional pop. So Lemnisgate and Ace Zero can become a double pop.**\"\n   WHICH ACE: `Azalea` (destroy 1 card) or `Zeke` (banish 1 face-up monster). \"if you're going against a deck where they WANT their cards banished, **you prioritize going into Azalea, which would pop their card**.\" The gate: \"we do not have three spells in our graveyard, so if we went into Azalea she would send herself to the graveyard. So **it is more ideal to go into our classic Zeke**.\" — **`Azalea` only sticks at 4+ Spells in the GY.**\n4. **Then `Raye` again.** `Raye` in the GY revives whenever a face-up Ace LINK monster you control is destroyed by battle or leaves the field by an opponent's effect, and the revived Raye can tag out on their turn: \"we can use Raye's effect once again... and we can go into **Kagari if we wanted to grab an Engage for follow-up**... however, **if your opponent had a lot of gas, bounce our Raye and go into our new Link-1, Prototype Sky Striker Ace Amatsu**.\" THE DECISION: **Kagari when you need cards next turn; Amatsu when you need one more interruption right now.** You can often have both — \"if you did not want to go into Kagari, go into an Amatsu, which is another negate. Then on the Amatsu Special Summon you could banish your Lemnisgate, using your Roze as Link material, and go into a Kagari, still grabbing your Engage, and now we get the negate as well.\"\n5. **THE CAMELLIA LOOP** — the best repeating interaction this list has. End your turn with `Camellia` on board and `Linkage!` Set. On their turn: \"**use Linkage sending away Camellia to go into your Ace Zero, which gets you the pop with Ace Zero, but then also gets you the Camellia disruption** with sending away your opponent's monster to the graveyard. And the Camellia loop being able to do that every single turn — because at their end phase, if they did not get rid of the Camellia, it comes back to your turn.\" From a tournament report: \"Right away, use Linkage, send Camellia to the graveyard, brought out my Ace Zero. **Camellia then sent their [boss] to the graveyard, and now they had a Camellia on their field that they were unable to get rid of.** Especially against a deck [that runs] no link monsters, **Camellia is guaranteed to come back to your field**, so you're going to have a body to combo off.\"\n6. **`Linkage!` is also your hand-trap dodge.** \"we go into Kagari, use Kagari's effect to grab Engage back from the graveyard, our opponent drops an Infinite Impermanence targeting our Kagari. We have Linkage in hand, we **chain Linkage sending away Kagari, going into Ace Zero — then Kagari resolves grabbing us Engage**, and Infinite Impermanence no longer has the targeted Kagari. **So they basically went neg one.**\"\n   THE ADVANCED VERSION — `Linkage!` does NOT target its send and picks it at RESOLUTION: \"we set the card, use Linkage. Our opponent thinks we'll be sending away this set card... and **this is where the Widow Anchor comes in, cuz we can chain our Widow Anchor, negate their monster and take it** — and then when Linkage finally resolves, **you send away the taken card.** ... Linkage does need the main monster zone to be clear when it activates, however **because Widow Anchor is chaining to Linkage, it doesn't matter that a monster ends up being in the main monster zone.**\"\n   AND it recovers a negated summon: \"Solemn Judgment negates the summon of Ace Zero and gets destroyed... use a spell card, chain Linkage, **we can bring out a second copy of Ace Zero** because Solemn Judgment negated the summon of the first one.\"\n7. **`Lemnisgate!`'s first effect is defensive as often as offensive.** Target an equal number of Aces and \"Sky Striker\" Spells in your GY, shuffle them back (Link monsters return to the EXTRA Deck — engine-verified), then for every 3 shuffled cards return up to 1 card on the field to the hand.\n   - Vs `Lightning Storm`/`Harpie's Feather Duster`: \"put three Sky Striker Ace monsters and three Sky Striker spells back into your deck, then **bounce your Linkage and your Widow Anchor back to your hand**. These either get used or they get popped. **It's a one-for-two trade, better than a one-for-four.**\"\n   - Vs `Called by the Grave`: \"if our opponent tries to Called by the Grave any of these three cards [Zero / Kagari / Raye], **chain our Lemnisgate, putting the targeted card back into the deck or extra deck, invalidating your opponent's Called by the Grave.**\"\n   - Vs an opposing `Shark Cannon`: \"chain Lemnisgate, put back all of your monsters, and all of a sudden **their Shark Cannon is dead**.\"\n   - As a late-game STARTER with `Roze` in the GY: \"**if your opponent has a monster in the extra monster zone**, use Lemnisgate... then bounce the extra monster zone monster, then **Roze can resolve, special summon out to the field**\" (Roze's GY clause: an opponent's EMZ monster leaving by YOUR effect revives Roze AND negates a face-up monster).\n   - And it refuels the Deck for `Pot of Desires`. Ryan: \"two Lemnisgate — also for Desires, of course.\"\n\n=== GOING SECOND / BREAKING BOARDS ===\nRyan takes both sides depending on matchup: he chose to go SECOND in both duels of the 2024 YCS Niagara finals, and chose to go FIRST in Duel 3 of the 2026 WCQ Top 4. His stated blind-second philosophy from the 2024 profile is important and counter-intuitive: \"**the way that you play this deck [against a full combo deck] is you let them full combo, don't interrupt them, then you crack their board and they have zero follow-up**, because they commit most of their deck and extra deck into the board. But if you [hand-trap] them and then they pass, and then you set up two Widow Anchors, they're just going to kill you next turn because they still have all the resources. **So you actually need them to play.**\"\nAnd going first against combo he considers favourable: \"**it feels like when you go first against [Kewl Tune] or Branded, it's like you're playing a combo mirror where they can't hand trap you as well as you can hand trap them**... what are they going to do? Impermanence or the other hand traps are just terrible [against you]. Whereas you just have spells, or even regular engine, you can break their board. So it's a combo mirror where you're a little bit favored.\"\n- **`Azalea` is the going-second card.** \"**Azalea is not only not cuttable in Sky Striker, it is going to be one of the most used cards, especially if you're going to be going second**... it requires two LIGHT and/or DARK monsters, that's it, doesn't even require any Sky Striker cards... **a lot of the times going second, you sometimes just go into Azalea just to clear up your main monster zone.**\" It pops twice: \"we got our first pop when it was special summoned, then in the battle phase [we] banish one spell from the graveyard and destroy that opponent's monster. **So we're popping two cards with Azalea.**\"\n- **`S:P Little Knight` — make it BEFORE any `Linkage!`.** \"link off our Roze into any of our Link Ones that isn't Kagari, and then use both of those to link into S:P Little Knight — and **because we used a Link Monster, now S:P Little Knight can banish one of our opponent's cards.**\" Its banish reaches EITHER GY (your only main answer to a graveyard engine). Its Quick Effect banishes 2 face-up monsters including one of yours until the End Phase — a dodge and a removal at once.\n- **`Camellia` as a board breaker:** anything that sends it to the GY removes an opponent monster — \"getting a second effect because Camellia was sent to the graveyard, [handing] your opponent the Camellia, and then sending that monster to the graveyard.\"\n- **`Hayate` — know both modes.** Crash: \"crash our Hayate into our opponent's monster, we lose 1,500 life points, and then Hayate's effect [sends] `Raye` to the graveyard, and Hayate blows up... now Raye triggers, special summons out of the graveyard, then tags itself out into Ace Zero.\" Better if you can win the battle: \"battle over the monster with Hayate, and before that monster is destroyed send a copy of `Roze` to the graveyard... then **Roze can trigger in the graveyard, special summon, and on the special summon negate an additional monster.**\" Hayate is also your Engage-substitute under `Droll`: \"attack directly, send Shark Cannon to the graveyard, link off your Hayate into Kagari, grab Shark Cannon from the graveyard, activate it.\"\n- **The OTK skeleton off one `Raye`:** Raye -> `Zero` (2000) -> Zero searches `Linkage!` -> attack -> `Linkage!` sends Zero -> `Kagari` (re-adds `Linkage!`) -> attack -> `Linkage!` again -> another Ace -> attack. \"**because Linkage is not once per turn**, we send away Kagari, and from here we can go into any of our Sky Striker monsters.\" Ryan's own caveat about this deck's damage: \"this deck doesn't really OTK — it'll crack the board but it won't end the game... **that's really why it's important to play at least one Roze, so you can make the Linkage go for game.**\"\n- **Sequencing `Evenly Matched` (side):** \"you go into the battle phase. They think you're going to be swinging over with your Ace Zero. **You use Ace Zero's effect to tag out, and THEN you chain your Evenly Matched** — now your opponent has to banish everything besides one card, and when Ace Zero resolves it brings out Raye plus Roze and **pops the last card that your opponent had**.\"\n\n=== EXTRA DECK, CARD BY CARD ===\n- **`Sky Striker Ace = Zero` x3 — Link-2, \"2 Sky Striker Ace monsters\", NO \"Must be Link Summoned\".** Your best card: Raye tags into it, `Linkage!` cheats it out, it is the only Link-2 Ace you can Special Summon. On summon add 1 \"Sky Striker\" **Spell** from Deck **or GY**. Quick Effect: Tribute -> Special Summon `Raye` AND `Roze` from Deck and/or GY -> destroy 1 card. ONE Zero effect and one Zero summon per turn.\n  Search from the GY when you can: \"**I like to grab Engage from the graveyard as opposed to grabbing the third Engage from the deck** — pro tip, just because it's better to draw into Engage.\" It plays under `Droll`: \"**Ace Zero can also play under Droll because when it's special summoned it can add a Sky Striker spell from your GRAVEYARD.**\"\n  ⚠ \"Cannot be used as Link Material\" — it dead-ends your climb; move it with `Linkage!` or its own Tribute.\n  ⚠ DON'T PANIC-FIRE IT: \"your opponent tries to negate your Ace Zero and you freak out and tag it out to pop nothing. **Sometimes it's better, if you have Raye in the graveyard, to NOT activate your Ace Zero. Let it get negated.** Then if your opponent crashes over it, it triggers Raye; Raye brings itself back and tags out into another Ace Zero.\"\n  ⚠ HOW YOU LOSE: \"when you make Zero and use Zero's effect on board, you tribute it, and it gets Ashed or Belled or negated. **That's how you lose the game, because you're putting all of your resources into one card.**\"\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Kagari` x3 — Link-1, \"1 non-FIRE Sky Striker Ace monster\".** On Special Summon add 1 \"Sky Striker\" Spell from the GY; +100 ATK per Spell in your GY; one per turn. Every Spell you spend is one Kagari hands back. Ryan's discipline is the aggressive one — burn Kagari to guarantee follow-up, because there is another copy.\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Shizuku` x1 — Link-1, \"1 non-WATER Sky Striker Ace monster\".** Opponent's monsters lose 100 ATK/DEF per Spell in your GY; in the End Phase (if Summoned that turn) add 1 \"Sky Striker\" Spell from Deck with a name different from the cards in your GY. The End Phase search chains into itself: \"**End phase Shizuku grabs you Shark Cannon. Shark Cannon can chain in the same end phase**, take your opponent's Ace Zero from their graveyard, and then Ace Zero also resolves in the end phase, grabbing an Engage for follow-up. Or Ace Zero grabs a Lemnisgate, and **still the same end phase you resolve your Lemnisgate.**\" Her pool SHRINKS as more Spell names hit your GY — use her early. Ryan on the count: \"you only ever need one Shizuku to win the game if it resolves.\"\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Hayate` x1 — Link-1, \"1 non-WIND Sky Striker Ace monster\".** Attacks directly; after it battles, send 1 \"Sky Striker\" card from Deck to GY. A tempo/consistency Ace — a Battle-Phase Foolish Burial that feeds Kagari and the 3+ threshold. Never a Turn-1 play (no Battle Phase). Ryan on why it matters at all: \"**Hayate is good for sending spells, but it's actually more important just for chip damage, because it helps you put them in the range where you can OTK them later.**\" Its arrow points down into your own MMZ, occasionally why you pick it.\n- **`Prototype Sky Striker Ace - Amatsu` x1 — Link-1, \"1 Sky Striker Ace monster\" (ANY Attribute, unlike the other Link-1s).** Quick Effect: when an opponent's 2000+ ATK monster activates its effect, that effect BECOMES \"Destroy 1 'Sky Striker Ace' Link Monster your opponent controls\" — a negate that costs you an Ace, and an Ace dying to an opponent's card effect is exactly `Raye`'s GY revival trigger.\n  A top player's take is that the ATTRIBUTE matters more than the effect: \"Amatsu honestly matters way more than Camellia for the pure Sky Striker deck... **its attribute is really important because it's a DARK monster** — you go Zero, summon Raye and Roze, the Raye tags out into a Kagari, and now you have a LIGHT and a FIRE, which means you cannot make Azalea; but you can **turn the Kagari into an Amatsu, which makes it so you can actually make Azalea.** ... that's honestly the most relevant thing about this card.\" It is also the DARK that switches on `Linkage!`'s +1000.\n  Second effect nobody uses: \"when an attack is declared involving this card and an opponent's monster, target one Sky Striker monster you control and one card your opponent controls, destroy them. **So your Amatsu gets popped, and then you can pop one of your opponent's floodgates.**\"\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Camellia` x1 — Link-2, \"2 Effect Monsters\", MUST FIRST BE LINK SUMMONED.** Generic materials, so a stolen monster + anything makes it. Once per turn, **only while you have 3 or LESS Spells in your GY**, send 1 \"Sky Striker\" card from Deck to GY. If sent to the GY: target 1 opponent monster, Special Summon Camellia to THEIR field and send that monster to the GY; control returns to you in the End Phase.\n  ⚠ THE ORDERING TRAP: \"**you have to make sure you do Camellia's effect while you have three or less spells** — because if you used a bunch of spell cards before going into Camellia, we would not be able to send Engage to the graveyard.\"\n  WHAT TO SEND: \"either send `Raye` just to get it in rotation, because there will be times where Ace Zero tags itself out and your opponent has an Ash Blossom or some interruption — **Raye in the graveyard is really important for the grind game** — or send a copy of `Lemnisgate!`, only if you run two copies. **If you're playing the Desires version, you 100% should be playing two copies of Lemnisgate.**\" (This list runs exactly two.)\n  Honest counterweight: \"Camellia in the pure build honestly doesn't come up that much — sometimes I summoned it twice in seven rounds. But when it does come up it's nice: an extra spell in the graveyard to make sure your Engage draws; **you can use Talents to take [a monster], Widow Anchor to take more freely, and then turn those bodies back into Sky Strikers**, whereas previously you'd always have to make Zeke.\"\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Zeke` x1 — Link-2, \"2 monsters, including a Sky Striker Ace\", MUST BE LINK SUMMONED.** On Link Summon: banish 1 face-up monster until your opponent's next End Phase. Once per turn: target 1 other card you control, gain 1000 ATK and send that card to the GY.\n  ⭐ THE NON-OBVIOUS LINE: **banish ZEKE ITSELF** to empty your Main Monster Zone and switch your Spells back on. \"our Zeke banishes itself, so that way we **cleared up our main monster zone and we're able to use cards like Engage**... and it comes back at the end of our opponent's turn.\" Same trick recycles a spent `Zero`: \"Zeke on special summon can banish your Ace Zero... during your opponent's end phase Ace Zero comes back.\" Ryan (2024): \"**one Zeke is super super standard in Striker, especially good against any deck where you're trying to play a back-and-forth grind game — being able to banish one of their cards for turn is just amazing, and it helps you bridge links.**\"\n  Its second effect is a free Spell into the GY, or a way to trigger `Camellia` from the field.\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Azalea` x1 — Link-2, \"2 LIGHT and/or DARK monsters\", MUST BE LINK SUMMONED.** On Special Summon: destroy 1 card; **then, if you have 3 or LESS Spells in your GY, it sends ITSELF to the GY.** Battle: banish 1 Spell from GY to destroy the monster it battles. Ryan's own note from 2024, which applies here (27 Spells): \"**because my deck has [so many] spells my Azalea pretty much never has to destroy itself when I use the [summon] effect, so you can use this to clear two bodies a lot of the time with both of its effects.** And it's extremely convenient to make because LIGHTs and DARKs are prominent... **if you have Widow Anchors and Shark Cannons but you bricked so you have no engine, you can link into Striker engine with this.**\"\n- **`S:P Little Knight` x1 — Link-2, \"2 Effect Monsters\".** See going-second above. ⚠ NOT a \"Sky Striker Ace\": `Linkage!` cannot summon it, `Lemnisgate!` cannot Link Summon it, and a resolved `Linkage!` locks it off for the turn. \"**every time you draw Linkage this card just can't be summoned, but whenever you don't have Linkage this card is a pretty neat option.**\" It replaced `Super Starslayer TY-PHON` between YCS Columbus and the WCQ.\n- **`Albion the Branded Dragon` x1 and `Ecclesia and the Dark Dragon` x1 — you NEVER summon these.** They are the only two legal \"monster that mentions Fallen of Albaz\" targets for `The Fallen & The Virtuous`. See below.\n\n=== THE NON-SKY-STRIKER PACKAGE ===\n- **`The Fallen & The Virtuous` x3 (Quick-Play, always treated as a \"Branded\" and \"Dogmatika\" card).** Ryan: \"**this card is completely ridiculous in this deck. The fact that it replaces itself, lets you dodge Impermanence and Veiler, and is a quick play for Vision, and lets you chain to Engage to get more spells in grave. This card is phenomenal.**\" A Reddit thread on his win agreed: \"TF&TV does a lot of work for the deck.\"\n  - EFFECT 1 (the default): send `Albion` or `Ecclesia and the Dark Dragon` from your Extra Deck to the GY, then destroy 1 face-up card on the field. There is ALWAYS a legal target, because your own face-up Spell counts.\n  - **ORDER MATTERS. Send `Albion` FIRST.** \"**One of them is absolutely mandatory — it's Albion the Branded Dragon... the goal is the first usage of The Fallen & The Virtuous is always going to be sending Albion so that you can set another copy of The Fallen & The Virtuous.**\" Because Albion was sent to the GY that turn, in the End Phase it adds or Sets 1 \"Branded\" Spell/Trap from your Deck — and this card IS a \"Branded\" card. ENGINE-VERIFIED (Albion in GY -> End Phase -> another `The Fallen & The Virtuous` out of the Deck).\n  - **SEND `Ecclesia` SECOND.** Its GY effect then targets `Albion` (a Level 8 Fusion) in your GY or banishment plus 1 card on the field and shuffles all three into the Deck — spin removal that also puts BOTH fodder pieces back in your Extra Deck. The whole package described end to end: \"**Our first copy sends [the first target], which gets another copy of TF&V, then that second free copy sends Ecclesia, which reshuffles [the first] and another card on field. In essence, one copy of the card represents, over 2-3 turns, 2 pops, +1 card, and then a shuffle.**\" And: \"**as long as you can shuffle back the Fallen & Virtuous, you basically have an infinite loop where every single turn you get to destroy your opponent's cards and shuffle back cards.**\"\n  - **IT IS AN IMPERMANENCE/VEILER DODGE.** \"If you went into Kagari and were trying to grab Engage back, but your opponent had a targeting negate like Effect Veiler or Infinite Impermanence, **you would use [this] to send Albion to the graveyard and pop your own Kagari. So now the Effect Veiler or Infinite Impermanence cannot resolve, because Kagari is no longer on the field — and you did not lose [the card] because you're going to get another copy.**\" The same works to protect your own Spell from a targeted negate: \"you can destroy your own spell cards that are being targeted... to make them resolve.\"\n  - Why it beat `Super Polymerization` for the slot in every competitive Striker list: \"**it was Super Poly until the night before... but we looked at our starting patterns and it was bad into everything else. This is just a lot better into everything.** This also fixes one of the main problems with the deck, which is **if you get hand-trapped on one of the [starters] it's really hard to extend.**\"\n  - You may only activate 1 `The Fallen & The Virtuous` per turn, and it SENDS FOR COST — it is dead under `Dimensional Fissure`/`Macro Cosmos`.\n- **`Radiant Typhoon Vision` x3 + `Mystical Space Typhoon` x1.** Vision: ●draw 2, then discard a \"Radiant Typhoon\" card or a Quick-Play Spell — **or your ENTIRE hand if you have neither**; ●add 1 `Mystical Space Typhoon` from Deck or GY. Each effect is once per turn BY NAME, so across three copies you get exactly one draw-2 and one MST-fetch per turn. There are no \"Radiant Typhoon\" monsters here, so the discard is always a Quick-Play Spell — i.e. a free Spell into the GY. TCGplayer's summary: \"**Radiant Typhoon Vision is basically an Allure of Darkness for all Quick-Play Spells.**\"\n  MST re-Sets it (\"If this card is destroyed by the effect of `Mystical Space Typhoon`: You can Set this card\") and Vision fetches MST back out of the GY, so the pair recurs: \"**you can even chain your MST to your Vision if you want to reset it.**\" TCGplayer adds the hidden use: \"**This effect can sometimes be timed to Chain block and prevent your opponent from responding to your other cards.**\" MST's other job is the sourced \"chain MST to pop your own `Engage!` so Engage is its own third Spell\".\n  ⭐ **`Vision` PLAYS UNDER `Droll & Lock Bird`, and your searches do not.** Fire it in the DRAW PHASE when you fear Droll: \"**make sure to use your effects like Vision in the Draw Phase to avoid Droll & Lock Bird**\" — Droll cannot be activated in the Draw Phase, and Vision DRAWS rather than adding from the Deck. Ryan says it in one line: \"**Vision under Droll is very good.**\"\n  WHEN IT IS BAD (sourced, and worth knowing before you side): \"**If you're going second, you want to be drawing hand traps instead of drawing a card that can draw into hand traps... in hand-trap-heavy formats and matchups where hand traps are necessary, you'll not want to have a copy of this card.**\" And it gets BETTER post-side, \"because the value of your non-engine card increases after siding.\"\n- **`Pot of Desires` x3.** Banish 10 face-down, draw 2, one per turn. Ryan (2024, on the same count): \"**this does suck to draw multiples. I drew raw three Desires in my hand twice... but even with all that I would still play three, because this card is one of the best cards to start your turn. It helps you play through Ash, because when you're baiting through negates you just want as many cards that say 'draw' as possible — that way if they have an Ash it means one more of your cards will resolve.**\" THE COST IS REAL: three copies can banish 30 of a 40-card deck. `Lemnisgate!` at 2 is the counterweight.\n- **`Forbidden Crown` x2 (Quick-Play).** Apply to 1 face-up monster until end of turn: negate its effects, it cannot attack, cannot be destroyed by battle or effects, is unaffected by activated card effects except its own, cannot be Tributed, cannot be used as Fusion/Synchro/Xyz/Link material. **Neither player can activate monster effects in response to its activation.** Konami's own tech article: \"**it essentially boils down to the monster being stuck on the field without an effect!** ... while your opponent can still use cards like Solemn Judgment to negate Forbidden Crown, **they likely won't have a chance to respond if they haven't already established a board.**\"\n  - GOING FIRST IS ITS BEST MODE. \"**Throughout my testing this card is actually much better going first than second.** If your opponent normal summons [their starter] and you Crown that, **not only did you negate it, but you stopped it from being used as link material. They need very specific extenders to play past that.** Also, **if they summon a monster in the EMZ and you Forbidden Crown that, they're probably just screwed** — they can't get the monster out of the EMZ.\" Ryan's own reason for keeping it: \"mainly for [Kewl Tune]... **going second against a lot of decks it's not that good, so you typically side it out going second in every matchup that's not [Kewl Tune]. But most matchups you can keep it in going first.**\"\n  - IT DOES NOT TARGET — you choose the monster on RESOLUTION. \"sometimes your opponent can dodge it by [sending] one of their cards, or using Fallen & the Virtuous to pop their own card... there's one game where there were two monsters on board, my opponent went Crown and I chained something to remove my card and **they had to Crown their own monster**.\"\n  - ⭐ IT IS ALSO A DEFENSIVE CARD, on YOUR monster. Konami: \"**Crown's effects even make it so that common ways to get rid of problematic monsters, such as Super Polymerization or [a] Kaiju, aren't able to get rid of a monster affected by Forbidden Crown.**\" And: \"**if you have all the follow-up but you're worried about dying... you can just Forbidden Crown your own monster, you basically are not gonna be able to die, especially if the monster's in defense. And then you guarantee get to live a turn.**\"\n  - ⚠ THE COUNTERPLAY YOU MUST KNOW: \"**if you Forbidden Crown a monster, it can't be destroyed by battle but you can still ATTACK it.** ... If [a 0-ATK Link] gets hit by Forbidden Crown, it's very easy for your opponent to just keep attacking you over it and just kill you that way. **Sometimes summon your monsters in defense so your opponent doesn't Forbidden Crown a low attack monster and kill you over it.**\" Since your Aces are 1500-2000 ATK, assume the mirror will try this on you.\n  - ⭐ SECOND JOB — IT SHUTS OFF `Droll`. \"commonly when you're going second you use an effect to search, and on resolution you can use [a Quick-Play that monster effects can't answer]... **you can do the same thing with Forbidden Crown — Forbidden Crown has an extra use in that you can also do this GOING FIRST.** Let's say I go [starter], search. On resolution I can use Forbidden Crown, hit my own [monster], and then they can't Droll me.\" Caveat from the same source: \"in practice it doesn't come up that much, because Forbidden Crown is so good going first that I generally just want to save it.\"\n  - What the mirror does to YOU: \"**This card destroys Sky Striker. If you apply it to a Raye, that's pretty tough.** Sky Striker does have ways around it... while they have Raye, none of the Sky Striker quick-plays can be activated, but **if they have any other quick-play they can then chain the Raye after.** ... **If you wait against Sky Striker until they put something in the EMZ, then they can chain Linkage to it.**\"\n- **`Triple Tactics Talent` x1.** Live only if the opponent activated a monster effect during YOUR Main Phase — the reward for being hand-trapped. Draw 2 / take a monster until the End Phase / look at their hand and shuffle 1 away. Taking a monster puts it in your MMZ: convert it into `Camellia` (2 Effect Monsters) or `Zeke`/`S:P` the same turn. Ryan on why 1 and not more (2024, when he ran 2): \"**this card kind of turbo-draws through itself... you really don't want to be drawing into multiples of cards that are dead.**\"\n- **`Shark Cannon` x1.** Banish 1 monster from their GY, or at 3+ Spells Special Summon it to your field (it cannot attack, and it lands in your MMZ — Link it off). Ryan: \"Some people don't run this. **I run it because I thought it was important for Branded**... a lot of people seem to think Sky Striker loses to Branded but I think it's actually pretty easy. **You just have to grind them.**\" His 2024 note on its range: \"it has random uses against everything: against [a grind deck] I was using this to banish their grind game; against [a combo deck] I was **stealing [their omni-negate boss] all weekend with this, and then you take it as an omni negate.**\"\n- **`Called by the Grave` x1 / `Reinforcement of the Army` x1.** RotA fetches `Raye` or `Roze`. Called by the Grave (2024 note): \"**negates [their boss], negates Ash, negates Droll**\" — and note the reverse, that THEIR Called by the Grave is the scariest card against you: \"if your opponent uses Called by the Grave on any of these cards [Zero, Kagari, Raye] mid to late game, **your game is pretty much cooked**.\" Your `Ghost Belle` answers it, because Belle negates effects that banish a card from the GY.\n\n=== HAND TRAPS: YOURS AND THEIRS ===\n- **`Ash Blossom` x3** and **`Ghost Belle` x1** are both there to stop the format's key push. Ryan: \"Belle — this was kind of a fun slot. It's not completely illogical. **You kind of treat it like a fourth copy of Ash** because it does a lot of the same things... typically what I do against [Kewl Tune] is, **if my hand is bad I'll just slam it on the first turn if I draw this. If my hand is good, or I can pair it, I'll maybe save it for later** — or save it for [their turn-3 push], especially late game.\" His explanation of why he wants BOTH names: their key card summons a Tuner \"from hand, Deck, or GY\", so Ash covers the Deck and Belle covers the GY, and unlike a Veiler-style negate they cannot dodge by chaining it away — \"**having Ash and Belle to negate the [push] in a way where they can't interact by dodging... is extremely important.**\"\n- **`Ghost Ogre` x2** — his stated targets were Branded and the Ritual deck; explicitly weak against the format's best deck. Do not fish for it in a matchup where nothing sits face-up on the field waiting to activate.\n- **`Droll & Lock Bird` x2** — your own anti-search card, and the deck it hurts most is a searching deck. You are ALSO weak to it. A top pilot, bluntly: \"**this deck is very bad into Droll and Lock.**\"\n- **PLAYING THROUGH `Droll` YOURSELF:** (1) fire `Radiant Typhoon Vision` in your DRAW PHASE, before Droll has a window; (2) after a search resolves, use turn-player priority to chain `Forbidden Crown` (monster effects cannot be activated in response), closing Droll's window; (3) `Zero` still works — it adds from the **GY**; (4) `Hayate` sends from Deck to GY and `Kagari` retrieves from the GY, which never touches \"add from Deck to hand\".\n- **PLAYING THROUGH `Infinite Impermanence` / `Effect Veiler`:** they target, so make the target vanish. (1) chain `Linkage!` and send the targeted Ace away — its effect still resolves; (2) chain `The Fallen & The Virtuous` and destroy your own targeted Ace; (3) chain-block with `Roze` — \"we go into Raye, link summon off Raye into Kagari, then on summon of Kagari we chain our Roze to special summon. **This chain-blocks Kagari from grabbing Engage** — if our opponent had a card like Ghost Belle... Roze special summons, Kagari resolves, grabs back Engage, and now we also have two bodies on board.\"\n- **BAITING ORDER:** activate the effect you can afford to lose first. \"your opponent will use [a negate] in response to Raye's effect, so you check for that right here. Then you activate Zero's effect on summon to add a card — **that checks for Infinite Impermanence and Veiler, because your opponent will want to chain to Zero to stop the search.**\" And: \"if we were going first, more than likely I'd **just bait out the Ash with Engage**.\" Ryan's version of the same idea: with this many draw Spells, \"**if they have an Ash it means that one more of your cards will resolve.**\"\n- **A REAL DECISION HE MADE**, from an analyst reviewing his rank-1 replays — what to dump with `Hayate`/`Camellia` when the opponent holds `Ghost Belle`: \"**He prioritized dumping Raye over Engage**... if I have Engage in grave, I summon Kagari, he Belles me, then I have nothing. **If I dump Raye, I activate Kagari, he Belles — well, I have Raye in grave.** ... I feel like a lot of Sky Striker players would have not accounted for that.\"\n- **`Nibiru`:** UNSOURCED for the defensive case. No source found for how Sky Striker plays THROUGH an opposing Nibiru. This deck rarely presents five summons in a turn, so treat it as a low-priority risk rather than building a plan around a claim nobody made.\n\n=== GRIND GAME AND RESOURCE MATH ===\nTrack FOUR numbers on every decision:\n1. **Spells in your GY** — 3+ for `Engage!`'s draw, `Widow Anchor`'s steal and `Shark Cannon`'s revive; 4+ for `Azalea` to survive her own effect; **3 or LESS** for `Camellia`'s mill. Camellia is therefore an EARLY-turn card and Azalea a LATE-turn card, and the two thresholds pull in opposite directions.\n2. **Aces left in your Extra Deck** — they never return except through `Lemnisgate!`.\n3. **`Raye`/`Roze` in your GY** — fuel for `Zero`'s tag out and the trigger for `Lemnisgate!`.\n4. **Cards left in your Deck** — three `Pot of Desires` is a real clock.\nThe four recursion engines: `Kagari` (Spell from GY), `Zero` (Spell from Deck OR GY), `Shizuku` (Spell from Deck, new name only), `Lemnisgate!` (Aces to the Extra Deck, Spells to the Deck). When you have a choice, take from the GY and leave copies in the Deck to draw. `Camellia` and `Zeke` both convert a card into a Spell in the GY when the count is the bottleneck.\n\n=== DECISION RULES (apply directly from the menu) ===\n1. A monster is stuck in your MMZ and you want a Sky Striker Spell -> Link it away FIRST (2 Effect Monsters -> `Camellia`/`S:P`; 2 incl. an Ace -> `Zeke`; 2 LIGHT/DARK -> `Azalea`; 1 Ace -> `Kagari`/`Shizuku`/`Hayate`/`Amatsu`), or Link Summon `Zeke` and banish Zeke itself.\n2. `Engage!` is live, you have fewer than 3 Spells in the GY, and you hold any Quick-Play -> activate `Engage!` first, then CHAIN the Quick-Play so Engage draws.\n3. Holding `Radiant Typhoon Vision` and NO other Quick-Play Spell -> do NOT use the draw-2; use the `Mystical Space Typhoon` search, or wait.\n4. Fear `Droll & Lock Bird` -> use `Vision`'s draw-2 in your DRAW PHASE; or chain `Forbidden Crown` with turn-player priority right after a search resolves.\n5. Going first with `Zero` summoned -> use the SEARCH now, keep Zero on board, save the Tribute for their turn. Never both — one Zero effect per turn.\n6. Their turn, they commit a monster -> `Widow Anchor` (negate only, if you still hold another Anchor) -> then `Zero`'s Tribute -> then `Lemnisgate!` off the Raye/Roze summon. Save the STEAL for your LAST Widow Anchor.\n7. Choosing the Ace off `Lemnisgate!`: 4+ Spells in GY -> `Azalea` (pop, and she stays). 3 or fewer -> `Zeke` (banish; Azalea would kill herself).\n8. Raye tags out on their turn -> `Kagari` if you need cards next turn, `Amatsu` if you need one more interruption now. With a `Lemnisgate!` in the GY you can often have both.\n9. Your Ace is targeted by `Infinite Impermanence`/`Effect Veiler` -> chain `Linkage!` (send it away) or `The Fallen & The Virtuous` (destroy it yourself). The effect still resolves; their card whiffs.\n10. Your `Zero`/`Kagari`/`Raye` in the GY is hit by `Called by the Grave` -> chain `Lemnisgate!` and shuffle that card away. Or `Ghost Belle` it.\n11. You want `S:P Little Knight` this turn -> make it BEFORE any `Linkage!` resolves.\n12. You want `Azalea` but your two bodies are LIGHT + FIRE (e.g. `Roze` + `Kagari`) -> convert the `Kagari` into `Amatsu` (DARK) first.\n13. About to Link `Camellia` -> confirm the GY Spell count is 3 or LESS, or you lose the mill.\n14. Ending your turn with `Camellia` on board -> Set `Linkage!` so their turn gives you a Zero plus a free removal.\n15. First use of `The Fallen & The Virtuous` -> send `Albion` (it re-sets another copy in the End Phase). Second use -> send `Ecclesia` (it later shuffles Albion + a field card + itself back).\n16. Opponent activates a monster effect during your Main Phase -> `Triple Tactics Talent` is now live.\n17. Deciding whether to Set another card -> stop at two. Hand traps survive `Lightning Storm`/`Harpie's Feather Duster`; Set Spells do not.\n18. You are ahead on resources and afraid of dying -> `Forbidden Crown` YOUR OWN monster (preferably in defence) to make it indestructible and un-Tributable for the turn.\n\n=== SIDE DECK (Ryan's own plan) ===\n- **`Mulcharmy Fuwalos` x3 + `The Winged Dragon of Ra - Sphere Mode` x2 — the going-second blowout package.** \"these are the go-second cards. If you're playing against a deck like [Elfnote or Power Patron], you kind of just need these blowouts. **This deck cannot be built like a normal hand trap deck, so you need cards that are high risk, high reward. That's what Fuwalos is — it might not resolve into Ash, but if it resolves it gives you a very good chance of winning.** ... [Sphere Mode] is pretty normal in decks that don't super need their normal summon. **It's something you can draw into off Fuwalos — that's why the ratio is 3-2. You just side out one `Raye`, one `Roze` and the `Reinforcement of the Army` when you put these in, so you don't ever have too many normal summons.**\" (His own 2024 warning about Mulcharmy still applies: it is a card that does nothing if Ashed, and \"if it gets ashed you pretty much lose on the spot against a competent deck\".)\n- **`Pot of Sloth` x3.** \"this card is completely ridiculous. It's actually like custom. **You put it in going second literally every match. It's just consistency.**\" A pro's framing: \"**what this card essentially reads is: you can choose your hand when you're going second**\"; and its limits: \"**this card is bad in hand trap decks**... it's clearly meant to be played in a deck that has [board breakers].\" And: \"**if your opponent has a spell lock you can't even attempt to activate it**\", plus \"**it lines up very poorly into Solemn Accusation**\" — remember that when the mirror sides its own Accusations against you.\n- **`Evenly Matched` x3.** \"super standard. It was mainly for the [Light and Darkness Ritual] deck, because their game plan is [Anti-Spell Fragrance] — and **if you get into a fair grind game with that deck you're guaranteed to lose. So our plan against it was to blind second post-side and then try to Evenly them.** Because if you're blinding second they won't have the floodgates in; and even if they do, there are a lot of cards you can draw in this deck to pair with the Evenly.\" It is also, per one pro, essentially the only out to a monster somebody `Forbidden Crown`ed.\n- **`Solemn Accusation` x3 + `Solemn Judgment` x1.** Ryan: \"**These cards are completely broken. Your deck has a problem with Lightning Storm and Feather Duster.** So if you can deep-draw into your deck for these post-side and then set them with your board, you pretty much always win.\" Another WCQ-level Striker pilot says the same thing and adds the key framing: \"**I wouldn't play Warning or Solemn Strike, cuz that's not why you're playing them. You're not playing them to stop their engine — you're using it so your interruptions don't get blown away.** And you draw heaps of cards going first, so it's usually draw at least one side card.\" Note Accusation can be activated the turn it is Set by revealing another Set Trap.\n- **What comes OUT:** 1 `Raye`, 1 `Roze`, 1 `Reinforcement of the Army` for the going-second monsters; `Forbidden Crown` going second outside the Kewl Tune matchup.\n\n=== MATCHUPS (NAWCQ 2026 metagame; 19 of the Top 32 were one deck) ===\n- **Kewl Tune (the deck to beat, roughly half of the Top 8 and Top 32; Ryan beat 11 of them):** Tuner-only Synchro combo where every monster searches on summon and disrupts when used as Synchro Material — including one that **banishes a random face-down card out of YOUR Extra Deck**, which is the literal \"clip insurance\" reason for 3 `Zero` + 3 `Kagari`. `Ash Blossom`, `Ghost Belle` and `Droll` are your best cards; `Ghost Ogre` and `MST` are your worst. Their kill plan is resolving their Level-4 Normal-Summon combo starter on turn 3 — \"**their goal is to make [it] resolve on turn three, because [it] is a huge push if it resolves. You're probably winning the game unless you have multiple good play spell sets to deal with [it] after the fact. So having Ash and Belle to negate [it] in a way where they can't interact by dodging is extremely important.**\" `Forbidden Crown` is mained for this matchup and is the one matchup you keep it in going second. Ryan's honest read: \"the deck definitely has a bit of a good matchup [here]. **I do think people overrated it a little bit. It's probably like 60/40.**\"\n- **Light and Darkness Ritual (Azamina Ritual):** their plan is `Anti-Spell Fragrance` plus a Quick-Effect tribute-negate boss. \"**if you kind of get into a fair grind game with that deck, you're guaranteed to lose.**\" Blind second post-side and `Evenly Matched` them before the floodgates land.\n- **Branded / Despia:** favourable, contrary to reputation. \"**a lot of people seem to think that Sky Striker loses to Branded, but I think it's actually pretty easy. You just have to grind them.** As long as you do a little bit on their turn one — maybe stop them with a hand trap — or your hand needs to be very above average. But they also struggle to beat you when you go first.\" `Shark Cannon` and `Ghost Ogre` are the main-deck concessions here.\n- **Elfnote and Power Patron (the heavy combo decks):** side into `Fuwalos` + `Sphere Mode` + `Evenly Matched` and go second. Remember the counter-intuitive rule: against a deck that commits its whole hand to a board, **let them combo and then break the board**, rather than hand-trapping them into passing with resources intact.\n- **The Sky Striker mirror:** `Widow Anchor` your OWN Link monster in response to theirs so they gain no body; `Lemnisgate!` to invalidate their `Called by the Grave` and `Shark Cannon`; their `Forbidden Crown` on your `Raye` locks you unless you hold a non-Sky-Striker Quick-Play; and copy counts decide it.\n- **Any deck with no Link Monsters:** the `Camellia` loop is unbreakable for them — they cannot remove the Camellia you hand them, and it returns to you every End Phase.\n\n=== WEAKNESSES ===\n1. **`Droll & Lock Bird`.** \"this deck is very bad into Droll and Lock.\" Outs: `Vision` in the Draw Phase, `Zero` adding from the GY, `Hayate` + `Kagari`, turn-player-priority `Forbidden Crown`.\n2. **`Called by the Grave` on `Zero`/`Kagari`/`Raye`** — \"your game is pretty much cooked\" without `Lemnisgate!` or `Ghost Belle`.\n3. **Anything that forces a monster into your Main Monster Zone** — including your own `Widow Anchor`/`Shark Cannon`/`Triple Tactics Talent` steals and your own `Roze`. Everything switches off until you Link it away.\n4. **All-in on one `Zero`.** Committing your hand into a single Zero effect and getting it negated is the stated way this deck loses.\n5. **Deck-out and self-banish.** 3 `Pot of Desires` in a 40 is a real clock; the engine fuzz-test in this repo decked this list out under random play. `Lemnisgate!` is the only refill.\n6. **Extra Deck attrition.** Aces that leave never come back on their own, and the format actively banishes them out of your Extra Deck. Play them aggressively but count what is left.\n7. **`Lightning Storm` / `Harpie's Feather Duster` / `Anti-Spell Fragrance`** if you over-Set. That is the entire reason this list is a hand-trap deck.\n8. **Being attacked over your own Crowned monster.** A Crowned monster cannot be destroyed by battle but CAN still be attacked; do not Crown a low-ATK body in attack position while behind.\n\n=== SOURCING NOTES AND KNOWN GAPS (do not fill these in from intuition) ===\n- The decklist is confirmed twice over: Konami's official \"North America WCQ Top 32 Deck Lists\" (as \"Ryan Linus ON Yu - 1\") and the YGOPRODeck tournament record. Brian Chen finished 9th with a card-for-card identical 40/15/15.\n- Ryan Yu's quotes come from YouTube AUTO-CAPTIONS of his own NAWCQ-2026 winner's deck profile and of his 2024/2022 profiles. Sentence-level wording is reliable; CARD AND ARCHETYPE NAMES ARE OFTEN GARBLED, and names in [brackets] are decodings cross-checked against the actual decklists and the event's own archetype records.\n- His transcript refers to two `Mystical Space Typhoon` where the recorded list has one; the list here follows the official decklist.\n- UNSOURCED, derived from card text and verified only in this engine: (a) using `Lemnisgate!`'s GY effect to Link Summon `Camellia` specifically — every human source names `Zeke` or `Azalea`; (b) `Amatsu`'s negate deliberately destroying your own Ace in order to trigger `Raye`'s GY revival.\n- NO SOURCE FOUND: how to play through an opposing `Nibiru`; any first-hand report of whiffing `Radiant Typhoon Vision` and discarding a whole hand.\n- ENGINE NOTE (this repo only, not a rules point): the vendored card scripts crash on a `Duel.RegisterEffect` made from inside a resolving effect, so three moves are REFUSED by the engine rather than executed — resolving `Droll & Lock Bird` at all, destroying your own `Sky Striker Mobilize - Linkage!` while it is resolving (with `The Fallen & The Virtuous` or `Mystical Space Typhoon`), and occasionally `Sky Striker Ace - Zeke`'s Link-Summon banish. Pick a different target for the destroy; the rest of the deck is unaffected. Details in the comment header of this deck file.\n- Sky Striker lines published elsewhere that use `Hornet Drones`, `Multirole`, `Area Zero`, `Forbidden Droplet`, `Upstart Goblin`, `Super Starslayer TY-PHON`, `Pillar of the Future - Cyanos` or `Combined Maneuver - Engage Zero!` DO NOT APPLY — none of those cards are in this 40 or this Extra Deck. Where such a line is quoted above it is quoted for its PRINCIPLE, not as something you can execute.",
